{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the failed-PR path falls through on an implicit invariant — make the non-resume branch explicit**

```javascript
if (!prResult.success) {
    var prOutcome = recoverPrCreationFailure(params, ctx, prResult);
    if (prOutcome.resume) return action(params);
}
// execution continues here with a FAILED prResult
assignAndMoveToReview(ctx);
postPRCommentToJira(ctx.ticketKey, prResult.prUrl, branchName);
```

Today this is safe only because `recoverPrCreationFailure` always ends the non-resume path with `throwPrCreationFailure(...)` — i.e. the correctness of `action()` depends on an invariant inside another function, with no local guard. If that helper is ever refactored (e.g. someone makes it return a reset-result object like `recoverGitOutcome` does, which is a plausible parallel), execution silently continues with `prResult.success === false`: the ticket gets assigned, moved to In Review, commented with a bogus `prResult.prUrl`, and labelled — precisely the "green no-PR leg" gh-729 exists to prevent. One line closes the hole:

```javascript
if (!prResult.success) {
    var prOutcome = recoverPrCreationFailure(params, ctx, prResult);
    if (prOutcome.resume) return action(params);
    throw new Error('unreachable: recoverPrCreationFailure must throw when not resuming');
}
```

(or have the helper itself return the throw-marker and rethrow it, matching `rethrownAsIs`.) Compare with the gate-failure paths just above, which return a result explicitly rather than relying on a callee-side throw.
